## Maintaining a Healthy Repository (Do's and Don'ts)

<div class="portal-table-wrap">
  <table class="portal-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 50%;">DO (Engineering Best Practices)</th>
        <th style="width: 50%;">DON'T (Strictly Prohibited)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Use feature branches:</strong> Isolate all changes in dedicated branches.</td>
        <td><strong>Commit directly to master or develop:</strong> Direct pushes to shared branches are blocked.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Keep PRs small:</strong> Limit PRs to &lt;400 lines of code for fast, thorough reviews.</td>
        <td><strong>Keep long-running branches:</strong> Branches open &gt;5 days suffer severe merge conflicts.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Merge frequently:</strong> Ship small, verifiable increments into <code>develop</code>.</td>
        <td><strong>Skip code reviews:</strong> Never bypass peer reviews, even for small fixes.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Run automated testing:</strong> Ensure all local unit tests pass before opening a PR.</td>
        <td><strong>Merge failing builds:</strong> Never override or bypass broken CI checks.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Review all code changes:</strong> Inspect every file diff carefully before approval.</td>
        <td><strong>Create monolithic PRs:</strong> Avoid giant, 1000+ line PRs that cannot be properly audited.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Follow naming conventions:</strong> Use standard prefixes (<code>feature-</code>, <code>bugfix-</code>, <code>hotfix-</code>).</td>
        <td><strong>Leave commented-out code:</strong> Remove dead code before submitting PRs.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

